[{"category":"section","location":"#Penalized-splines","page":"Penalized splines","text":"Use the same spline basis to fit the simulated EEG with and without a smoothing penalty. The penalized fit estimates one smoothing strength per predictor using REML.","title":"Penalized splines"},{"category":"section","location":"#Setup","page":"Penalized splines","text":"<details><summary>Install the packages</summary>\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.activate(mktempdir())\nPkg.add([\n    Pkg.PackageSpec(name=\"Unfold\"),\n    Pkg.PackageSpec(url=\"https://github.com/unfoldtoolbox/UnfoldSim.jl\",\n                    rev=\"994f253c2fa0d918357c94180b206732e0b7b2ae\"),\n    Pkg.PackageSpec(url=\"https://github.com/Mango-Wang2024/UnfoldPenalizedSplines.jl\"),\n    Pkg.PackageSpec(name=\"CairoMakie\"),\n    Pkg.PackageSpec(name=\"UnfoldMakie\"),\n])\n\n</details>\n\nusing Unfold\nusing UnfoldSim\nusing UnfoldPenalizedSplines\nusing CairoMakie, UnfoldMakie","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"#Simulate-EEG-with-a-nonlinear-effect","page":"Penalized splines","text":"Use UnfoldSim to set the P300 amplitude to 5 + x₁ + 0.2x₁². The second predictor, x₂, is generated after the EEG, so its true effect is zero.\n\np3 = (\n    p300(; sfreq = 100),\n    @formula(0 ~ 1 + continuous + continuous^2),\n    [5, 1, 0.2],\n    Dict(),\n    0,\n)\ndata, evts = UnfoldSim.predef_eeg(; noiselevel = 8, p3 = p3)\nevts.x1 = evts.continuous\nevts.x2 = rand(UnfoldSim.MersenneTwister(1), size(evts, 1))","title":"Simulate EEG with a nonlinear effect"},{"category":"section","location":"#Fit-the-spline-models","page":"Penalized splines","text":"Fit both models over the same window, from −100 to +500 ms relative to each event.\n\nformula = @formula(0 ~ 1 + pspl(x1, 8) + pspl(x2, 8))\nbasis = firbasis(τ=(-0.1, 0.5), sfreq=100; interpolate=false)\n\nunpenalized = Unfold.fit(UnfoldModel, formula, evts, data, basis)\npenalized = fit_pspl(formula, evts, data, basis)","title":"Fit the spline models"},{"category":"section","location":"#Plot-the-marginal-effects","page":"Penalized splines","text":"Predict the EEG at 21 values of x₁ while holding x₂ at 0.5. Each color represents one x₁ value.\n\ngrid = Dict(:x1 => collect(range(-2, 2; length=21)), :x2 => [0.5])\neffects_combined = vcat(effects(grid, unpenalized), effects(grid, penalized);\n                        source=:method => [\"Unpenalized\", \"REML\"])\n\nplot_erp(effects_combined;\n         mapping=(; y=:yhat, color=:x1, group=:x1 => nonnumeric, col=:method))\n\n(Image: Predicted EEG responses across x₁ from the REML and unpenalized fits, with x₂ held at 0.5)","title":"Plot the marginal effects"},{"category":"section","location":"#Adjust-spline-smoothing","page":"Penalized splines","text":"Both sliders start at the REML estimates. Move either one to change how much its spline can bend across predictor values. The left plot varies x₁ at x₂ = 0.5, and the right plot varies x₂ from 0 to 1 at x₁ = 0.\n\n<iframe id=\"spline-smoothing-demo\" src=\"assets/smoothing-demo.htm?v=pluto\" title=\"Adjust the smoothing strengths of the x1 and x2 splines\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"display: block; width: 100%; height: 720px; border: 0; background: white; color-scheme: only light;\"></iframe>\n<script src=\"assets/embed-demo.js\"></script>\n\nDemo fitting · Demo plots","title":"Adjust spline smoothing"}]
